Z||000005||tgowens||11-04-2001||02:32 AM||||Yes, Pike, you have one. It is located behind the carpet inside the right side of the trunk. It turns on when the radiator fan comes on and blows (sometimes) air out of the two pipes mounted on the front side of the trunk wall to cool the coil and the alternator.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.whgraphics.com" TARGET=_blank>West Highland Graphics & Design</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Dan010||11-04-2001||11:11 AM||||Now this bring up another question,<BR>the rad. fan does not come on till temp is over 235, if the cooling system is working fine, the temp will never get that high, so the engine bay fan will never come on, is this correct?? Even though the engine bay may see temp. that would benifit from the engine fan being turned on, so does this mean that a manual switch woudl be good, and or a lower temp switch be added for both fans?????||||reg||10
Z||000007||theogre||11-04-2001||02:31 PM||||In my cave, Read the heat management and under the engine menu, fan stuff. This will give you some ideas how to better utilize the V6 auxilary fan. Running the fan more may reduce the ignition problems that plague the V6.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
